---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navbar from '@/components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '@/components/Footer.astro';

// Posts that tell these stories properly. The colophon points; it doesn't retell.
const reading = [
  {
    href: '/blog/migrating-portfolio-nextjs-sanity-to-astro',
    title: 'Migrating my portfolio from Next.js + Sanity to Astro 5',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/gdpr-safe-by-construction',
    title: 'GDPR-safe by construction: German-market sites with nothing to consent to',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/self-service-photo-pipeline',
    title: 'A self-service photo pipeline: git-based CMS + Cloudinary on a static Astro site',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/three-darkrooms',
    title: 'Three darkrooms: shooting digital, thinking in film',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Colophon — how this site is built"
  description="What ercan-atak.de runs on: Astro 5, four Svelte islands, Cloudinary, and no cookie banner because there is nothing to consent to."
>
  <Navbar />

  <main
    class="
      px-4 pt-4 pb-6 md:px-6 md:pt-6 md:pb-7 lg:px-9 lg:pt-9 lg:pb-7
      max-w-[1320px] mx-auto
    "
  >
    <p class="text-[11px] text-mute-2 tracking-[0.18em] uppercase mb-2 font-mono">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-phosphor transition-colors">// home</a>
      &nbsp;/&nbsp;colophon
    </p>

    <article
      class="
        prose prose-sm prose-invert max-w-3xl
        prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:tracking-[-0.02em]
        prose-h1:text-paper-hi prose-h1:text-2xl
        prose-h2:text-paper prose-h2:text-base prose-h2:mt-8 prose-h2:mb-2
        prose-p:text-paper-2 prose-p:leading-[1.6] prose-p:text-[13px]
        prose-li:text-paper-2 prose-li:leading-[1.6] prose-li:text-[13px]
        prose-a:text-phosphor prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline
        prose-hr:border-ink-3 prose-hr:my-10
        prose-strong:text-paper
      "
    >
      <h1>Colophon</h1>

      <p>
        This site is a project like the others, so here is what it runs on.
        It began as Next.js 14 with Sanity as the CMS and was rebuilt on
        Astro, mostly to stop shipping a framework to readers who only
        wanted to read.
      </p>

      <h2>Build</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Astro 5</strong>, static output, with View Transitions
        between pages. Almost everything is a server component that ships
        no JavaScript at all. Four things genuinely need a browser, and
        those are <strong>Svelte 5</strong> islands: the mobile menu, the
        live clock, the reticle cursor, and the contact form. Styling is
        <strong>Tailwind</strong>, dark only — there is no light theme and
        no toggle, which is a design decision rather than an unfinished
        one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Content lives in the repository, not a hosted CMS. Five Astro
        content collections — blog, work, lens, paints, testimonials —
        each validated by a <strong>Zod</strong> schema at build time, so a
        malformed entry fails the build instead of the page. Writing is
        MDX. A combined RSS feed spans four of the five.
      </p>

      <h2>Photography</h2>

      <p>
        Images are served from <strong>Cloudinary</strong>, watermarked by
        a transformation rather than by hand, and stripped of their
        metadata on delivery — no GPS, no camera serial, no software
        fingerprint in anything you can download. The captions still show
        full EXIF, because that is read server-side from the stored
        original at build time. Rolls and albums are edited through a
        git-based CMS that commits straight to the repository, so
        publishing a set of photos is a commit like any other.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>

      <p>
        There is no cookie banner because there is nothing to consent to.
        The site sets no cookies, and uses no LocalStorage or
        SessionStorage at all. Analytics is <strong>GoatCounter</strong>,
        which is cookieless and stores no personal data. Fonts are
        self-hosted rather than loaded from Google's CDN — a German court
        decided in 2022 that the CDN version is an unconsented transfer of
        visitors' IP addresses to the US, and opportunistic claim letters
        followed. Email addresses and the postal address on the legal
        pages are encoded in the markup and assembled in the browser, so
        they never appear as literal strings in the HTML a scraper fetches.
      </p>

      <p>
        Security headers are set at the edge, secrets are scanned on every
        commit and every push, and the form posts to a third-party endpoint
        that never stores a message.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it runs</h2>

      <p>
        Hosted on <strong>Vercel</strong>, DNS at Cloudflare, domain
        registered at Porkbun, mail at <strong>mailbox.org</strong> in
        Berlin — chosen so that no message leaves the EU on its way to me.
      </p>

      <h2>Longer versions</h2>

      <p>
        Four posts cover most of the above at proper length:
      </p>

      <ul>
        {reading.map((r) => (
          <li>
            <a href={r.href}>{r.title}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
